.TH ECOLAB 2
.SH NAME
ecolab: run, parse, equilibrium \- Danby two-species models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "ecolab.m";
ecolab := load Ecolab Ecolab->PATH;

err := ecolab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, ex, ey, scale, err) := ecolab->parse(spec);
(x, y) := ecolab->equilibrium(m, ex, ey);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ecolab
is the last of the five viewer libraries, for the Danby two-species
family: a phase plot, an equilibrium marker, a time series and the
controls, as a library the model calls.
.PP
.EX
ecolab->run(ctxt, "cooperation", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
The vocabulary is
.IR dynsys (2)'s
\(em
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.B eq
\(em plus
.BR title ,
.B manual
and two of ecolab's own:
.TP
.BI equilibrium " xexpr " , " yexpr"
The model's own fixed point, marked on the phase plot.  Required: it is
the thing this family exists to show.  Like a state's initial value it
may use the PARAMETERS only \(em
.B eq
lines are bound per step, and a fixed point does not depend on where the
system happens to be \(em so a guarded denominator has to be written out
rather than named once.
.TP
.BI scale " num"
A fixed maximum for both phase axes.  Without it they follow the
populations.
.PP
Exactly two
.B state
lines are required; their names are the phase plot's axis labels.  The
viewer clamps both at zero after every step, as this family always has.
.PP
.B Parse
returns the model, the equilibrium's two coordinate expressions and the
fixed scale (zero if none), or an error string.
.B Equilibrium
evaluates those two expressions; the parameters must already be bound,
which
.B dynsys->initialstate()
does.
.IR Ecolabtest (1)
uses both to check that each model is actually at rest at the point it
marks.
.PP
The window is Tk's.  The parameter sliders are Tk scales, the buttons are
Tk buttons, the readout is a Tk label, and the layout is a Tk geometry
manager's; the phase portrait and the time series share one
.IR panel (9)
widget holding an image the library draws into.  A click in it arrives as
a Tk binding with coordinates already relative to the panel, and
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B invert()
turns those into species numbers through the same scales that drew them,
so the trajectory starts where the pointer is.  It did not always: the
program this library replaced took the first species from the vertical
position and the second from the horizontal, while the phase plot drew
the first species along x, so a click started the system at the transpose
of the point under it.
